<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inconfidência Mineira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Minas Conspiracy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Inconfidência Mineira was a conspiracy for independence from Portugal among officers, priests, poets and landowners of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, in 1789. Angered by heavy gold taxes and inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, they planned a rising for the day of a new tax levy. Betrayed before it began, the plotters were arrested, and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiradentes">Tiradentes</a> was hanged in 1792.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira.jpg" alt="Inconfidência Mineira" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Oswald · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_da_Inconfid%C3%AAncia_1789_Os_Inconfidentes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Gold mining in the captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> was in decline, and the miners found it ever harder to pay the Portuguese crown its tax of one-fifth of the gold. When the captaincy could not satisfy the royal demand, it was burdened with an additional tax, the derrama, by which the government meant to enforce the payment of all debts. The conspirators were largely of the white upper class; many had studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, especially at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, and some owed large debts to the colonial government. Besides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, they drew on the French philosophers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The plot drew in military men, priests and intellectuals, among them the poets Cláudio Manuel da Costa and Tomás Antônio Gonzaga, the lawyer Alvarenga Peixoto, the priest José da Silva de Oliveira Rolim, the philosopher and chemistry student José Álvares Maciel and Lieutenant Colonel Francisco de Paula Freire de Andrade of the regiment of dragoons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiradentes">Joaquim José da Silva Xavier</a>, known as Tiradentes, an alferes from Andrade's regiment, was its most enthusiastic propagandist.</p><p>The conspirators lacked well-formed plans and an overall leader. Some were republicans, others monarchists; some favoured the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, others thought it impractical. Many wanted a republic with an elected leader, its capital at São João del Rei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a> a university town, with property and slave ownership kept intact. They also proposed promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, exploiting iron and saltpeter, encouraging large families and creating a citizens' militia. Three participants revealed the plans, among them Joaquim Silvério dos Reis, and the rebels were arrested in 1789.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The trials lasted from 1789 to 1792. Freire de Andrade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiradentes">Tiradentes</a>, Maciel and eight others were condemned to the gallows and seven to perpetual banishment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; Peixoto ended his life in exile at Ambaca in Portuguese Angola. Queen Maria I commuted the death sentences to banishment except where the circumstances were aggravated, as with Tiradentes, who took full responsibility. He was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> on 21 April 1792, and the pieces of his body were displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Vila Rica</a> where he had spread his ideas.</p><p>The anniversary of his death is a national holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In 1963 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> adopted the flag designed by the conspirators as its state flag, with a triangle inspired by the Holy Trinity and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> motto taken from Vergil's Eclogues.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inconfidência Mineira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the end of the eighteenth century, leaving the hinterland stagnant, and the Crown's intention to enforce payment of all debts, the derrama, drove the white upper class of Minas Gerais into the <b>Inconfidência Mineira</b> of 1788–89: a republic with an elected leader, but with property and slavery intact.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As gold declined, a conspiracy, the <b>Inconfidência Mineira</b>, plotted independence in 1789; its leader Tiradentes was hanged in 1792.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-minas-gerais">Captaincy of Minas Gerais</a></li></ul></section>
